# Copy to .env.local.
#
# These are SERVER-side only — deliberately not NEXT_PUBLIC_*. Anything with that prefix is
# inlined into the browser bundle, and an Aperture API key grants full access to an org's
# episodes. The dashboard reaches the backend through app/api/aperture/[...path]/route.ts,
# which attaches the key where the browser cannot read it.
APERTURE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000

# --- Sign-in mode ---
# Leave APERTURE_API_KEY EMPTY for per-org mode (recommended): each person signs in with their
# own organization's API key, which is sealed (AES-GCM) into an HttpOnly cookie and attached by
# the proxy. One deployment then serves every customer. Requires APERTURE_SESSION_SECRET in
# production; locally a development-only secret is used.
#
# SET it for single-org mode: every request uses this one key, and APERTURE_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD
# (below) gates the dashboard.
APERTURE_API_KEY=

# Sentry (optional, free tier). Leave blank locally — error capture stays off until set.
# The DSN is not a secret, so this one is legitimately public.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=
SENTRY_DSN=
# Set in Vercel CI for source-map upload (not needed to run):
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
# SENTRY_ORG=
# SENTRY_PROJECT=

# --- Dashboard sign-in ---
# Single-org mode only: a shared password gating the whole dashboard, including the API proxy.
# Unset in development means no sign-in; in production an unset password makes the dashboard
# refuse every request rather than serve fleet data to anyone with the URL.
# This is a pilot-grade gate, not per-user identity — put SSO in front of it before you need to
# know *who* did something. See DEPLOY.md.
APERTURE_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD=
# Signs (single-org) or encrypts (per-org) the session cookie. Required for per-org mode in
# production — use a long random value (`openssl rand -hex 32`). In single-org mode it defaults
# to the password; set it separately so rotating the password and invalidating live sessions are
# independent decisions. Changing it signs everyone out.
APERTURE_SESSION_SECRET=
